const description = {
  //A Row
  "AI in Shipping":
    "Artificial intelligence uses smart computer systems to analyze data and make decisions. In shipping, it helps plan faster routes and reduce fuel costs. It can predict delays before they happen. AI tools improve tracking and communication. It reduces human errors and improves customer satisfaction. Overall, it makes shipping quicker, safer, and more efficient.",
  "API Integration":
    "API integration connects different software systems so they share information instantly. It avoids manual data entry and reduces mistakes. Tracking numbers, rates, and updates flow automatically between systems. This saves time and improves accuracy. It helps shipping partners work together smoothly. Businesses can grow faster with connected systems.",
  "Airport-to-Airport":
    "Goods are flown from one major airport to another airport only. The shipping company only handles the air transport part. The receiver manages customs clearance and final delivery. This service usually costs less because fewer tasks are included. It works best for experienced buyers or local logistics teams. It is used for fast and long-distance deliveries.",
  "Airport-to-Door":
    "Goods are shipped from the origin airport and delivered to the receiver’s address. The logistics provider handles customs clearance and local delivery. It is more convenient because the customer does not need to arrange transport. It costs more due to added services. It is widely used in e-commerce and personal shipments. It ensures complete delivery to the final destination.",
  "Algorithmic Pricing":
    "Computer models automatically calculate shipping prices based on real data. It considers weight, demand, distance, and market conditions. Customers receive quick and accurate quotes online. It reduces errors and delays in pricing. Rates change faster when the market changes. It helps companies stay competitive and fair.",
  "All Risk Coverage":
    "A strong cargo insurance that protects against most loss or physical damage. It covers many risks during transportation or storage. Some exclusions may still apply based on packaging or special situations. It gives shippers financial protection and peace of mind. It is recommended for high-value or delicate goods. It supports quick recovery if damage occurs.",
  "Amendment Charges":
    "Extra fees charged when shipping information changes after booking. It covers the work needed to update documents and systems. Wrong names, weights, or cargo details often cause amendments. Planning correctly helps avoid these charges. They are common in air and ocean shipping. Accurate data reduces cost and delay.",
  "Asset Utilization":
    "Using trucks, ships, and containers in the best possible way. High utilization reduces empty trips and waiting time. It lowers operating costs and improves productivity. Better planning helps move more goods with the same assets. It benefits both shipping companies and customers. It is a key measure of logistics efficiency.",
  "Audit Trail":
    "A record that shows who made changes to shipment data and when. It helps find errors and ensure the information is correct. It supports security and prevents fraudulent edits. Audit trails make compliance checks easier. They improve transparency in logistics operations. They build trust between partners and authorities.",
  "Authorized Economic Operator (AEO)":
    "A certification from customs that shows a company follows rules and keeps cargo secure. AEO companies get faster clearance at borders. They face fewer inspections and delays. It improves their reputation in global trade. It increases security throughout the supply chain. It helps goods move smoothly across countries.",
  "Auto-population":
    "Forms fill automatically with stored information. It reduces manual typing and saves time. It prevents repeated entry of the same data. Fewer mistakes occur in shipping documents. It helps finish bookings and labels faster. It makes digital logistics tools easier to use.",
  "Automated Commercial Environment (ACE)":
    "A system used by US customs to process import and export data electronically. It reduces paperwork and manual checks. Businesses can submit documents faster and avoid delays. It keeps shipping information in one place. It supports better communication between customs and traders. It makes trade more secure and efficient.",
  "Automated Reconciliation":
    "Software automatically matches shipment or financial records. It quickly finds missing or incorrect entries. It reduces long manual checking work. It improves accounting accuracy and reporting. Companies see their payments and balances faster. It helps maintain clean and reliable records.",
  "Automated Screening":
    "Systems check cargo details against rules and restricted lists. It alerts if a shipment has banned or risky items. It speeds up compliance checks that used to take longer. It reduces delays and penalties for mistakes. It protects supply chains from unsafe cargo. It ensures legal and secure trade.",
  "Autonomous Supply Chain":
    "A supply chain run mostly by technology rather than humans. It uses AI, robots, and sensors to manage operations. It predicts problems early and fixes them automatically. Goods move faster with fewer errors. It improves cost, speed, and safety in logistics. It represents the future of smart shipping.",
  //B Row
  "Bay Plan":
    "A layout that shows where every container is placed on a ship. It helps load and unload cargo in the correct order. It ensures the ship stays balanced and safe at sea. Planners use it to avoid delays at ports. It improves space usage on the vessel. It is essential for smooth container operations.",
  Benchmarking:
    "Comparing business performance with industry standards or competitors. It helps companies understand where they need improvement. Shipping firms compare cost, speed, and service quality. It supports better planning and decision making. Benchmarking encourages innovation and efficiency. It helps businesses stay competitive.",
  "Bid Bond":
    "A financial guarantee provided by a bidder in a shipping or logistics contract. It shows the bidder is serious and capable of doing the job. If they refuse the contract later, the bond covers the loss. It protects the buyer from unreliable bidders. It builds trust in contract bidding. It is common in large logistics projects.",
  "Blank Sailing":
    "When a shipping line cancels a scheduled voyage. It may happen due to low demand or port issues. Cargo might get delayed or rerouted. It helps carriers control costs and capacity. Customers need updated planning when blank sailings occur. It affects supply chain timing and visibility.",
  "Blockchain Documentation":
    "Using blockchain technology to store and share shipping documents digitally. It keeps records secure, accurate, and traceable. Documents cannot be changed without approval. It reduces paperwork and fraud in trade processes. It speeds up communication between all parties. It improves trust in global logistics.",
  "Blockchain Verification":
    "Proof checking of shipment data using blockchain’s secure network. Every transaction is recorded and verified by multiple parties. It prevents fake or altered information. It improves transparency in cargo movement. It makes customs and compliance faster. It supports safer trade operations globally.",
  "Bobtail Fee":
    "A charge applied when a truck moves without a container or trailer. It covers fuel and driver cost when driving empty. Bobtailing usually occurs between pickup and drop points. The fee helps trucking companies maintain profitability. Good planning reduces unnecessary bobtail trips. It is common in port and warehouse operations.",
  "Bonded Goods":
    "Imported items stored without paying customs duty right away. Duties are paid only when goods leave the bonded area. Businesses get more time to sell or process them. It improves cash flow and reduces risk. Bonded goods must follow strict security rules. Customs controls them until cleared.",
  "Bonded Warehouse Air":
    "A secure warehouse near an airport where imported goods are stored duty-free. Companies pay customs duties only when goods are released for local use. It helps manage stock before final delivery. It reduces immediate tax cost and supports re-export. It follows strict customs rules and monitoring. It is useful for international air cargo operations.",
  "Booking Confirmation":
    "A document or message that confirms a shipping reservation. It includes details like vessel, dates, cargo, and costs. It shows that space is secured for the shipment. Customers use it to arrange paperwork and planning. Any changes after confirmation may lead to fees. It is a key step in the shipping process.",
  "Break Bulk":
    "Cargo that is loaded and handled piece by piece instead of in containers. Examples include machinery, steel, or large equipment. It needs special equipment and more manual work. Break bulk shipping is used for oversized or heavy items. It can take longer and cost more than container shipping. It remains important for industrial goods.",
  "Break Bulk Point":
    "A location where bulk shipments are separated into smaller parts. From here, goods are sent to different final destinations. It reduces handling cost earlier in the journey. It improves distribution planning and transport efficiency. It is often used in large supply chains. It supports faster regional delivery.",
  "Bulk Booking":
    "A reservation made for transporting goods in large quantities. It is commonly used for materials like grain, coal, or chemicals. Bulk shipping often fills entire vessel spaces. It usually offers better pricing for high volume. Coordination is needed for safe loading and unloading. It supports industries that need large-scale supply movement.",
  "Bullwhip Effect":
    "A small change in customer demand causes bigger changes in orders along the supply chain. It leads to excess stock or shortages. Poor communication and delays increase the effect. Businesses try to reduce it through better forecasting. Clear data sharing helps stabilize supply flow. Managing it improves cost and customer service.",
  "Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF)":
    "A surcharge added to shipping rates to cover changing fuel costs. Fuel price fluctuations affect ocean carriers heavily. BAF keeps shipping companies protected from fuel risk. It is updated regularly based on global prices. Customers see BAF as a separate charge on freight bills. It maintains fairness in pricing when fuel costs rise.",
  "Business Intelligence Shipping":
    "Using data analytics to guide decisions in logistics. It helps track performance, costs, and cargo movement. Companies see problems early and improve planning. Dashboards provide clear reports to management. BI supports better customer service and reduced waste. It promotes smarter and faster business growth.",
  "Buyers Consolidation":
    "Combining goods from multiple suppliers into one shipment for a single buyer. It reduces shipping cost and space waste. Customs clearance becomes simpler with one set of documents. It improves timing for large orders. It helps importers manage stock better. Freight forwarders usually arrange the consolidation process.",
  //C Row
  "Container Depot":
    "A storage and handling facility for empty or loaded containers. It is used before shipment or after delivery. Containers are repaired, cleaned, and inspected here. Truckers pick up or return containers to the depot. It helps manage container supply near ports and cities. It supports smooth container logistics operations.",
  "Container Flow":
    "The movement of containers across the supply chain. It tracks how containers travel between ports, ships, depots, and customers. Smooth flow avoids delays and reduces extra costs. Poor flow leads to container shortage or pile-ups. Companies monitor flow to balance supply and demand. It improves efficiency in global trade.",
  "Container Insurance":
    "Insurance that protects containers from damage or loss. It covers risks while moving or stored in depots and ports. Owners avoid high repair or replacement costs. It is important because containers are valuable assets. Rules define what damage is covered and what is not. It gives financial security in shipping operations.",
  "Container Pool":
    "A shared group of containers used by multiple companies. It reduces the need for each company to own large fleets. Pools improve container availability in high-demand areas. They lower repositioning costs and empty movements. Rules ensure fair use among partners. It supports better resource sharing in logistics.",
  "Container Security Initiative (CSI)":
    "A global program led by US Customs to check high-risk containers before they reach the United States. It improves screening at foreign ports. The goal is to prevent dangerous items from entering supply chains. Partner countries cooperate for stronger security. Inspections happen earlier to avoid port delays. It increases protection in international trade.",
  "Container Tracking":
    "A system that lets users follow a container’s location during transport. It uses numbers, GPS, or smart devices for updates. Customers know where their goods are at all times. It helps predict arrival time and avoid surprises. Tracking improves communication between logistics partners. It adds transparency and safety to shipping.",
  "Container Yard Cut Off":
    "The deadline for delivering containers to the port yard before a ship sails. Missing the cut-off means the container will not load on time. Clear timing avoids delays for customers. It helps the terminal plan stowage and paperwork. Shippers must schedule trucking carefully. It is a critical point in export operations.",
  "Contingency Insurance":
    "Insurance that covers unexpected events affecting shipments. It helps when another party’s insurance fails or refuses a claim. Shippers use it as a safety backup. It reduces financial risk in emergencies. It keeps cargo owners protected in uncertain situations. It adds confidence to global transport activities.",
  "Continuous Bond":
    "A customs bond used for multiple shipments throughout the year. It avoids paying a new bond for every import. It saves cost and time for frequent importers. It ensures taxes and duties will be paid to customs. Businesses get faster clearance at ports. It is widely used in international trade compliance.",
  "Continuous Improvement":
    "A long-term effort to make logistics processes better. Small improvements happen often instead of big changes rarely. It reduces waste, cost, and delays. Companies analyze data to find better ways of working. Employees take part in suggesting improvements. It builds a culture focused on quality and efficiency.",
  "Cost Optimization":
    "Finding the best balance between service quality and expenses. It reduces waste in transport, warehouse, and labor use. Good planning avoids paying for unnecessary operations. Technology helps choose cheaper and faster options. It improves profitability for shipping companies. Customers benefit from fair and stable pricing.",
  //D Row
  "Delivery Order (DO)":
    "A document issued by the carrier that allows the consignee to take delivery of cargo. It confirms all charges are paid and customs rules are completed. The receiver uses it to collect goods from the port or warehouse. It is a key document for final cargo release. Any delay in DO can delay delivery. It is one of the last steps in the import process.",
  Detention:
    "A fee charged when containers are kept outside the port longer than the free allowed days. It encourages quick return of empty containers. Longer holding increases cost for the shipper. Good planning helps avoid detention charges. It is common in import operations. Digital tracking helps manage container timelines.",
  "Digital Bill of Lading":
    "An electronic version of the traditional paper Bill of Lading. It proves ownership of cargo, contract of carriage, and receipt of goods. It is faster and safer because data cannot be easily lost or altered. Digital sharing reduces paperwork and delays. It improves transparency across the supply chain. It is becoming popular in modern shipping.",
  "Documentary Collection":
    "A payment method where banks handle shipping documents while collecting payment from the buyer. Banks do not guarantee payment, they only act as intermediaries. It is safer than open account but cheaper than a letter of credit. Documents are released only after payment instructions are met. It reduces risk for sellers in international trade. It follows strict banking rules.",
  Drayage:
    "Short-distance trucking that moves containers between port, rail, or warehouse. It is an important link in the door-to-door supply chain. Drayage is needed for both imports and exports. High congestion at ports often affects drayage time. It helps transfer cargo to long-haul transport. Efficient drayage reduces delays in logistics.",
  "Duties & Taxes Calculator":
    "A tool used to estimate customs charges for imported goods. It calculates tax based on product type, value, and destination rules. It helps shippers plan cost before the shipment arrives. It reduces surprises and delays at customs clearance. It supports transparency in international shipping expenses. It improves budgeting and pricing decisions.",
  "Duty Drawback":
    "A refund of duties and taxes paid on imported goods that are later exported again. It encourages manufacturing and re-export trade. Companies apply with proper proof and export documents. It improves cash flow and reduces cost in global business. Rules vary by country for eligibility. It supports competitive pricing for exporters.",
  "Dwell Time":
    "The amount of time cargo stays at a port, terminal, or warehouse before moving further. Longer dwell time leads to congestion and extra fees. Ports track dwell time to improve efficiency. Good planning and paperwork reduce waiting delays. Short dwell time improves supply chain speed. It is a key metric in terminal performance.",
  "Dynamic Routing":
    "A flexible system that changes delivery routes in real time. It uses live data such as traffic, weather, and demand. It helps trucks avoid delays and reach faster. It reduces fuel cost and increases customer satisfaction. Technology plays a major role in route planning. It keeps delivery operations smooth even when conditions change.",
  //E Row
  "Emergency Surcharge":
    "An extra fee added by carriers when unexpected costs rise. It is usually applied due to emergencies like port strikes or fuel price hikes. Shippers must pay it in addition to standard freight charges. It helps carriers manage sudden operational expenses. The amount may change based on the situation. It is temporary and removed once conditions become normal.",
  "Empty Container Repositioning":
    "The movement of empty containers to places where exporters need them. Some locations have too many empties while others do not have enough. Shipping lines reposition them to keep cargo moving. It increases cost since empty containers do not generate revenue. Good planning reduces the number of empty moves. It keeps global container supply balanced.",
  "End-to-End Visibility":
    "The ability to track a shipment from origin until final delivery. It gives real-time updates on status, location, and next steps. It helps companies plan better and avoid delays. Customers feel more confident when they can track their goods. Technology like GPS and sensors makes it possible. It improves supply chain control and decision making.",
  "Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR)":
    "A document confirming the condition of a container when it is handed over. It records damage, number, date, and who received it. It helps decide responsibility if the container gets damaged later. It is used at ports, depots, or truck handovers. It acts as proof during any dispute. It ensures accountability during container movements.",
  "Exception Handling":
    "A process to deal with problems that occur during shipping. Common issues include delays, lost documents, or damaged cargo. The goal is to fix the issue quickly and reduce impact. Alerts and tracking help detect exceptions early. Good handling protects customer commitments. It keeps the supply chain running smoothly even when issues occur.",
  "Expedited Freight":
    "A faster shipping option for urgent deliveries. It uses priority handling and direct routes to save time. It costs more than regular freight services. Mostly used for emergency shipments like spare parts or medical goods. It reduces risk of business loss from delays. It ensures the product reaches as soon as possible.",
  "Export Permit":
    "An official approval required to send certain goods out of a country. The government controls restricted items for safety and compliance. Without the permit, the shipment cannot move. Exporters must provide correct product details to get it. It prevents illegal or harmful goods from leaving the country. Rules differ based on commodity type.",
  "Express Air Service":
    "A very fast air shipping option used for time-sensitive cargo. Delivery usually happens in one or two days. It is ideal for small parcels and urgent goods. Real-time tracking keeps customers updated. It is a premium service so costs are higher. It reduces delivery time in international trade.",
  "Express Service":
    "A quick transport service for fast delivery needs. It can be done by air, road, or a mix of modes. It gets priority at every handling point. It is popular in e-commerce and urgent B2B shipments. Customers pay extra for speed. It improves satisfaction by shortening waiting time.",
  //F Row
  "Form A Certificate":
    "A document used to claim reduced customs duty under the Generalized System of Preferences. It proves that goods were produced in a developing country. It helps importers save money on taxes. Exporters must follow origin rules to qualify. Customs officers review the document at destination. It supports global trade participation of developing nations.",
  "Forwarders Cargo Receipt (FCR)":
    "A document issued by a freight forwarder confirming they received the cargo. It acts as proof of shipment for the seller. The forwarder takes responsibility to send goods as agreed. It is used for payment purposes, especially in trade agreements. It is not a document of title like a Bill of Lading. It improves trust between buyers and sellers.",
  "Free Time":
    "The allowed number of days cargo or containers can stay at a terminal without charges. It applies before detention or demurrage fees begin. Shippers use this time to complete customs and delivery. Longer free time helps avoid extra expenses. Ports and carriers set different policies. Planning well ensures free time is not wasted.",
  "Free Trade Agreement (FTA)":
    "A trade deal between countries that reduces taxes on goods. It makes importing and exporting cheaper and easier. Companies gain access to new markets with lower barriers. FTAs support business growth and job creation. Rules of origin must be followed to get benefits. It promotes strong economic cooperation between nations.",
  "Free Trade Zone (FTZ)":
    "A special area where goods can be stored, processed, or re-exported without full customs duties. Businesses use FTZs to delay or avoid taxes. It helps lower logistics costs and improve efficiency. It attracts international companies to invest. Activities inside FTZs follow strict regulations. It gives more control over supply chain expenses.",
  "Freight Aggregation":
    "A method of combining multiple small shipments to reduce cost. Forwarders pool cargo from different customers into one larger load. Bigger loads get better rates from carriers. It helps small businesses access affordable shipping. It improves space utilization in transport. It supports cost savings throughout the supply chain.",
  "Fuel Adjustment Factor":
    "A surcharge added to cover changes in fuel prices. It helps carriers manage cost fluctuations. The fee rises or drops based on the fuel market. It is common in both sea and air shipping. Shippers see it listed separately on invoices. It ensures transparency in freight pricing.",
  "Fumigation Certificate":
    "A document proving that cargo has been treated to remove pests. Some countries require it to protect their agriculture and environment. It is commonly needed for wood packaging and food products. A certified agency performs the fumigation. Customs officers check the certificate before clearance. It ensures safe and legal entry of goods.",
  //G Row

  "GDP Compliant":
    "A standard that ensures medicines and healthcare products are stored and transported safely. It protects product quality through controlled temperature and proper handling. Companies must follow strict guidelines to avoid contamination. Regular audits check compliance with rules. It is required for pharmaceutical shipments. It ensures patient safety in the supply chain.",
  "Garment on Hanger (GOH)":
    "A special shipping method for clothing already placed on hangers. It prevents wrinkles and reduces time needed for ironing. Racks or bars are installed inside containers or trucks. Fashion brands use it to deliver clothes store-ready. It improves product presentation at destination. It helps save labor and packaging costs.",
  Gateway:
    "A major hub in logistics where cargo is collected, sorted, and forwarded. It connects smaller locations with global routes. Airports and seaports often act as gateways. It helps speed up international distribution. Efficient gateways reduce transit time for shipments. It keeps supply chains connected worldwide.",
  "General Average":
    "A maritime rule that shares losses when cargo or ship is sacrificed to save the voyage. Every involved party contributes to the loss. It protects the shipowner during emergencies like fire or storms. A claim process calculates fair contributions. Cargo insurance often covers these costs. It ensures fairness in sea transport risks.",
  "Generalized System of Preferences (GSP)":
    "A trade program that reduces import tariffs for developing countries. It supports economic growth by making exports more competitive. Importers pay lower taxes when goods qualify by origin. Documentation must prove the goods meet rules. Many countries benefit from the scheme. It encourages global trade fairness.",
  Geofencing:
    "A digital boundary created using GPS technology. Alerts are triggered when cargo enters or leaves the zone. It improves security and shipment tracking. Companies use it to monitor truck routes and compliance. It helps prevent theft or unauthorized stops. It supports faster response to delays.",
  "Green Lane":
    "A faster customs processing channel for trusted and verified shipments. It reduces waiting time at borders and ports. AEO certified companies often get this privilege. It helps improve trade flow and reduce congestion. Only compliant cargo passes through smoothly. It supports secure and efficient trade operations.",
  "Green Logistics":
    "A set of practices to reduce environmental impact in transportation. It focuses on fuel efficiency, recycling, and lower emissions. Companies adopt cleaner vehicles and smart routing. It helps save cost while protecting nature. Customers prefer businesses that act responsibly. It supports sustainability in global supply chains.",
  //H Row
  "Handling Charges":
    "Fees for the physical movement of cargo within ports, warehouses, or terminals. These charges cover loading, unloading, lifting, and shifting goods. Costs depend on cargo type, size, and equipment used. Proper handling avoids damage and delays. It is a common part of total logistics cost. Transparency helps customers understand final pricing.",
  "Harmonized Commodity Description and Coding System (HS Code)":
    "A global system that classifies goods for customs and trade. Every product is assigned a code used for taxes, documentation, and regulations. Correct HS code selection avoids penalties or shipment delays. It helps governments track imports and exports. Businesses rely on it for accurate duty calculation. It ensures consistency in international trade language.",
  "Hazmat Air Transport":
    "The air movement of hazardous materials like chemicals or flammable goods. Strict safety rules control packaging, labeling, and documentation. Trained staff handle these shipments to prevent accidents. Airlines follow international hazmat guidelines. Extra checks and fees apply due to risk. It protects people and property during air travel.",
  "High cube container (40HC)":
    "A container similar to a 40-foot standard but with extra height. It allows more volume for light and bulky cargo. Warehousing equipment must match its taller size. Many exporters use it for furniture, machines, and textiles. It improves space utilization and reduces shipment count. Carriers often charge slightly higher rates for this type.",
  //I Row
  "ISF Filing (Importer Security Filing)":
    "A mandatory filing for US-bound ocean imports that shares cargo details before loading. It allows customs to assess security risks earlier. Missing or late filing leads to heavy penalties. Importers must submit information like shipper, buyer, and product details. It helps protect the supply chain from threats. It is also known as the 10+2 rule.",
  "Instant Quotation":
    "A fast online price calculation for shipping services. It helps customers know freight costs without waiting. Automated systems use cargo details and routes to create the quote. It improves decision making and booking speed. It reduces manual communication effort. It supports transparency and faster logistics planning.",
  "Institute Cargo Clauses":
    "A set of standard insurance terms used globally for marine cargo. It defines what risks are covered during transport. Options like A, B, and C offer different protection levels. Insurers and shippers follow these rules for clarity. It helps settle claims fairly when damage occurs. It brings consistency to international insurance contracts.",
  "Intermodal Shipping":
    "A transport method using multiple modes like truck, rail, and sea in one journey. Cargo stays inside the same container, reducing handling and risk. It improves efficiency for long-distance movement. It helps lower costs and fuel use. Smooth coordination between modes is important for on-time delivery. It supports flexible global logistics networks.",
  "IoT Tracking":
    "A technology that uses connected sensors to monitor cargo in real time. It reports location, temperature, and security status. Alerts notify users of delays or damage risks. It helps companies act quickly to solve issues. Customers gain better visibility of their shipments. It improves overall supply chain control and reliability.",
  //J Row
  Jettison:
    "The act of throwing cargo overboard to save a ship in an emergency. It helps stabilize the vessel during storms, fires, or flooding. Losses from jettison may be shared under General Average rules. Proper records are needed for insurance claims. It is a last resort decision by the ship’s captain. It protects the crew and the remaining cargo.",
  "Joint Rate":
    "A single freight rate agreed by two or more carriers for one shipment route. It avoids separate billing for each carrier involved. It simplifies cost estimation and documentation for shippers. Carriers share revenue according to their portion of the service. It supports smooth transportation across multiple networks. It is commonly used in intermodal and long-distance shipments.",
  //K Row
  "KYC Automation":
    "A digital process that verifies customer identities automatically. It reduces manual paperwork during shipping account creation or compliance checks. Automated tools check documents, business details, and risks. It helps prevent fraud and illegal trade activities. Faster verification improves onboarding for importers and exporters. It supports global trade security and regulatory compliance.",
  "Knot [Nautical]":
    "A unit used to measure speed at sea. One knot equals one nautical mile per hour. Ships and maritime weather reports commonly use this measurement. It helps plan travel time and fuel needs on ocean routes. Nautical miles relate to the Earth’s shape, improving navigation accuracy. It is a standard speed reference for all marine transport.",
  "Known Consignor":
    "A trusted shipper approved by aviation authorities. Their cargo undergoes less inspection because of strict security compliance. Approval requires audits of packing, handling, and facility safety. It speeds up airport screening and reduces delays. Air carriers accept these shipments with higher confidence. It helps protect the air cargo supply chain.",
  "Kyoto Convention":
    "A global agreement designed to simplify and modernize customs procedures. It promotes faster clearance and reduced paperwork for international trade. Countries that join must follow standardized rules. It improves transparency and cooperation between customs authorities. Businesses benefit from easier import and export processes. It supports secure and efficient global trade operations.",
  //L Row

  "Letter of Indemnity (LOI)":
    "A legal document in which a party promises to cover any loss or damage caused by a specific action. It is often used when shipping documents are not available but cargo needs release. The carrier receives protection from claims when accepting the LOI. It ensures business continues without long delays. Proper wording is important to avoid disputes. Banks may be involved for extra guarantee.",
  "Live Animal Transport":
    "The movement of animals by air, sea, or road under strict welfare rules. Special cages, ventilation, and temperature control are required. Trained staff handle loading and monitoring during transport. Health documents and inspections protect animal safety. Airlines follow IATA regulations for such cargo. It ensures humane treatment throughout the journey.",
  "Load List":
    "A document showing all containers planned to be loaded on a ship. It includes container numbers, weights, and destinations. Planners use it to balance the ship safely. The list must match customs and booking records. It helps avoid loading mistakes that cause delays. It supports safe and organized vessel operations.",
  "Load Optimization":
    "A strategy to use space efficiently in containers, ships, or trucks. It reduces empty space and lowers transportation cost. Software tools help calculate the best loading plan. It improves safety by balancing weight correctly. Higher efficiency supports sustainability goals. It ensures more goods move using fewer trips.",
  "Low Sulphur Surcharge (LSS)":
    "An extra fee to cover the cost of cleaner fuel required by environmental regulations. Ships use low sulphur fuel to reduce air pollution. Fuel price differences create higher operating expenses. Carriers apply LSS separately from base freight. The amount changes based on market fuel rates. It supports greener maritime transportation.",
  "Loyalty Program Benefits":
    "Rewards offered to customers who book repeatedly with the same logistics provider. Benefits may include discounts, free services, or priority support. These programs encourage long-term business relationships. Customers gain better pricing control. It strengthens trust and brand loyalty in competitive markets. Data insights help tailor offers to customer needs.",
  //M Row

  "Mother Vessel":
    "A large oceangoing ship that carries cargo on major international routes. It connects major ports where goods are transferred to smaller feeder vessels. Using mother vessels reduces shipping cost on long distances. They handle high volumes like containers, bulk, or oil. Efficient scheduling supports global trade flow. It forms the main backbone of maritime transport networks.",
  "Multi-currency Account":
    "A bank account that supports holding and paying in different currencies. It helps importers and exporters avoid exchange losses. Companies use it to manage global transactions smoothly. It reduces delays in cross-border payments. Businesses gain flexibility with international customers. It supports better financial planning in trade operations.",
  "Multi-modal Discount":
    "A cost benefit offered when a shipment uses multiple transport modes booked together. Carriers or forwarders provide better rates for combined services. It encourages efficient logistics planning across rail, road, sea, or air. Customers save money while keeping cargo moving smoothly. The discount depends on volume and contract terms. It supports integrated supply chain solutions.",
  "Multi-modal Tracking":
    "A system that monitors cargo movement through different modes in a single view. It gives real-time updates from origin to final delivery. Companies can quickly identify delays or route changes. Visibility across modes helps better planning and communication. Customers feel more confident with constant tracking. It improves reliability in long-distance logistics.",
  "Multimodal Shipping":
    "A shipping method using a single contract for multiple transport modes. One service provider takes full responsibility from pickup to delivery. Cargo stays in the same container, reducing handling risks. It saves time and cost for complex routes. Customs and documentation become simpler under one control. It supports smooth global movement of goods.",
  //N Row
  NVOCC:
    "A Non-Vessel Operating Common Carrier that provides ocean freight services without owning ships. It books space with carriers and then sells that space to customers. NVOCCs issue their own Bill of Lading and manage documentation. They offer flexible schedules and competitive pricing. Many shippers rely on them for better support and routing options. They play a vital role in global container logistics.",
  "Natural Disaster Coverage":
    "Insurance protection for cargo affected by events like floods, earthquakes, or storms. It helps recover financial loss when nature disrupts transportation. Shippers must check policy terms because some disasters may be excluded. It offers peace of mind for shipments to high-risk regions. Claims require proof of damage and proper paperwork. It supports business continuity during unexpected events.",
  "Natural Language Processing (NLP)":
    "A technology that helps computers understand human language. It enables chat-based booking, tracking queries, and document automation in logistics. Systems can read emails and extract shipment details automatically. It reduces manual typing and errors in communication. Customers get faster responses to shipping questions. It improves workflow efficiency across the supply chain.",
  "Next Flight Out (NFO)":
    "A premium service that sends urgent cargo on the soonest available flight. It is used for emergencies like machine parts or medical supplies. The shipment receives top priority at airports. It reduces downtime and business loss caused by delays. Higher cost reflects the speed and priority handling. It ensures the fastest possible air transport solution.",

  //O Row

  "On-board Courier (OBC)":
    "A person who personally carries urgent documents or small shipments on a commercial flight. They ensure secure and immediate delivery to the recipient. OBC is used for high-value or time-sensitive shipments. It avoids delays associated with regular cargo handling. Airlines provide special handling procedures. It guarantees faster and safe transport for critical items.",
  "One-way Lease":
    "A rental agreement for vehicles or containers that is not returned to the original location. It is commonly used when equipment needs to be relocated permanently. Costs may be higher due to positioning logistics. It simplifies operations for one-directional transport needs. Planning ensures availability at the destination. It is widely used in shipping and logistics fleets.",
  "Over Dimensional Cargo: Expert Handling for Special Loads":
    "Cargo that exceeds standard dimensions or weight limits. Specialized equipment and planning are required for safe transport. Permits and route surveys may be needed. Expert handling reduces risk of damage or accidents. Carriers use cranes, flatbeds, and escort vehicles. It ensures delivery of oversized or heavy goods efficiently.",
  Overage:
    "Extra quantity of goods delivered beyond the ordered amount. It can occur due to packaging errors or production variations. Companies track overage to avoid billing disputes. Some overage is acceptable under contract terms. It protects customer interests in critical supply situations. Clear documentation prevents confusion in shipment records.",
  //P Row
  "Pre-clearance":
    "Customs approval granted before cargo arrives at the destination. It speeds up release and reduces port congestion. Documentation is submitted and verified in advance. It lowers risk of delays and fines. Importers can plan deliveries more efficiently. Many airports and borders offer pre-clearance programs.",
  "Predictive Analytics":
    "Using historical and real-time data to forecast trends and outcomes. In shipping, it predicts delays, demand, or risks. Helps companies make proactive decisions. Reduces cost and improves service reliability. Tools identify potential supply chain issues early. It supports smarter logistics and planning strategies.",
  "Process Automation":
    "Using technology to perform repetitive tasks without human intervention. It speeds up documentation, tracking, and booking. Reduces errors and saves operational costs. Employees can focus on complex decision-making. Automation improves efficiency across the supply chain. It is widely used in warehouses and digital logistics platforms.",
  "Push Notifications":
    "Automatic alerts sent to users about shipment status or changes. They provide real-time updates on delivery, delays, or pickups. Improves customer engagement and communication. Reduces the need for manual tracking inquiries. Notifications can be sent via email, SMS, or app. Enhances visibility and control over shipments.",
  //Q Row
  Quay: "A dock or platform where ships load and unload cargo. It is part of the port infrastructure. Quays are equipped with cranes and storage facilities. Efficient quay management speeds up vessel operations. Safety and organization are essential during cargo handling. It forms a critical link in maritime logistics.",
  //R Row
  "Regional Value Content":
    "A calculation showing the percentage of a product made or processed in a specific region. Used to determine eligibility for trade benefits under FTAs. It helps importers reduce customs duties. Rules define which parts and processes qualify. It encourages local production and regional economic growth. Accurate documentation is required for compliance.",
  "Revolving Credit Facility":
    "A flexible loan that allows repeated borrowing up to a set limit. It is commonly used for working capital in trade finance. Borrowers repay and re-borrow as needed. Interest is paid only on the amount used. It ensures liquidity for importers and exporters. Banks manage it under agreed terms and conditions.",
  "Risk Mitigation Tools":
    "Methods or technologies used to reduce supply chain risks. Includes insurance, hedging, and safety stock strategies. Helps companies handle disruptions or financial losses. Improves reliability and customer satisfaction. Regular analysis identifies potential weak points. Supports proactive management of uncertainties.",
  "Roll on/Roll off (Ro-Ro)":
    "A shipping method where vehicles or wheeled cargo roll directly onto and off ships. It avoids cranes and heavy lifting. Common for cars, trucks, and construction machinery. Speeds up loading and unloading processes. Reduces handling damage risk. It is efficient for transporting large numbers of vehicles.",
  //S Row
  "Six Sigma":
    "A methodology to improve processes by reducing defects and variability. Used in logistics to increase efficiency and quality. Involves data analysis and continuous monitoring. Achieves better service consistency and customer satisfaction. Teams follow defined steps to identify improvements. Widely adopted for operational excellence.",
  "Substantial Transformation":
    "A concept in trade to determine the origin of a product. Goods are considered originating from a country if major processing or assembly occurs there. It affects eligibility for preferential tariffs under FTAs. Helps customs verify product origin claims. Rules define the percentage of value or operations needed. It supports fair trade practices.",
  "Sue and Labor":
    "A clause in marine insurance requiring the insured to take reasonable steps to prevent loss or damage. Efforts to protect cargo or ship can be reimbursed. Encourages proactive action during emergencies. Failure to act may reduce insurance claim. It ensures both insurer and insured share responsibility. Standard practice in cargo and vessel insurance.",
  "Supply Chain Resilience":
    "The ability of a supply chain to recover quickly from disruptions. It involves risk management, redundancy, and flexibility. Companies plan for natural disasters, strikes, or demand spikes. Resilient supply chains reduce losses and downtime. Technology and communication play key roles. It ensures consistent delivery of goods.",
  "Sustainable Supply Chain":
    "A supply chain designed to minimize environmental and social impact. Focuses on green transport, ethical sourcing, and resource efficiency. Reduces carbon footprint and waste. Companies follow regulations and sustainability standards. Benefits include cost savings and brand reputation. It supports long-term viability of operations.",
  //T Row
  "Temporary Admission":
    "A customs procedure allowing goods to enter a country for a limited time without paying duties. Goods must be re-exported within a set period. Used for exhibitions, samples, or temporary use. Documentation and guarantees are required. Helps companies save costs on short-term imports. Customs monitors compliance carefully.",
  "Total Loss":
    "A situation where cargo or vessel is completely destroyed or irrecoverable. Insurance covers financial compensation for total loss. Determining total loss requires survey and documentation. Partial salvage may still be possible. It triggers claims and legal procedures. It protects stakeholders financially in severe incidents.",
  "Transshipment Hub":
    "A central port where cargo is transferred from one vessel to another. Helps consolidate shipments for efficiency. Reduces shipping costs by combining routes. Allows smaller feeder vessels to access remote destinations. Hub management ensures timely and safe cargo transfer. It is a key element in global shipping networks.",
  "Trusted Trader Status":
    "A certification for companies that meet strict compliance and security standards. Offers faster customs clearance and reduced inspections. Encourages reliable and secure trade practices. Certification requires audits and continuous monitoring. Benefits include lower operational delays and cost savings. It builds credibility with authorities and partners.",
  "Turn Time":
    "The time taken for a vehicle or container to complete a trip and be ready for the next load. Shorter turn time improves fleet utilization. Planning, loading efficiency, and traffic affect turn time. Reducing delays saves cost and increases productivity. It is tracked to optimize transport operations. Key metric in trucking and port management.",
  //U Row
  "ULD Management":
    "The handling and tracking of Unit Load Devices in air cargo operations. Ensures containers and pallets are properly loaded and secured. Improves space utilization and flight efficiency. Reduces damage risk and cargo delays. Technology helps monitor ULD location and condition. Essential for smooth air freight operations.",
  "Unit Load Device (ULD)":
    "A container or pallet used to consolidate cargo in air transport. It allows efficient loading into aircraft cargo holds. ULDs reduce handling time and protect goods. They are reusable and standardized for airlines. Tracking and maintenance ensure safety. Critical for high-volume air cargo logistics.",
  "Usance Letter of Credit":
    "A financial instrument allowing payment to exporters at a future date after shipment. Provides credit time to importers while guaranteeing payment to exporters. Documents must comply with LC terms. Reduces risk for both parties in international trade. Banks facilitate the process. Helps manage cash flow effectively.",
  //V Row
  "Value Added Services (VAS)":
    "Additional services provided on top of basic shipping. Includes packaging, labeling, kitting, or quality checks. Enhances customer satisfaction and product readiness. Can differentiate providers in competitive markets. Often charged separately from standard freight. Improves overall logistics efficiency.",
  "Vendor Managed Inventory (VMI)":
    "A system where the supplier manages inventory levels at the buyer’s location. Ensures stock availability and reduces stockouts. Supplier monitors consumption and replenishes automatically. Improves supply chain efficiency and reduces administrative work. Collaboration between supplier and buyer is key. Supports just-in-time inventory practices.",
  "Vessel Sharing Agreements (VSA)":
    "An arrangement where multiple shipping lines share space on the same vessel. Reduces operating costs and increases service options. Carriers coordinate schedules and cargo allocation. Customers benefit from more frequent sailings. Agreements improve fleet utilization. Common in container shipping alliances.",
  "Volumetric Weight":
    "A calculation of cargo weight based on dimensions rather than actual weight. Used to determine freight charges for light but bulky shipments. Airlines and couriers use this to charge fairly. Volume weight ensures space usage is considered. Helps optimize revenue and container or aircraft capacity. Customers must provide accurate dimensions.",
  //W Row
  "War Risk Surcharge":
    "An additional fee for shipping cargo through conflict zones. Covers higher insurance and security costs. Applied on top of standard freight rates. Protects carriers and cargo owners from war-related risks. Surcharge depends on region and threat level. It ensures safe and financially covered transport.",
  "Warehouse Legal Liability":
    "Insurance coverage for goods stored in a warehouse. Protects against theft, fire, or accidental damage. The warehouse operator’s liability is defined under the policy. Limits and exclusions may apply. It gives confidence to owners leaving goods in storage. Helps settle disputes and claims efficiently.",
  "Weight Break":
    "A tiered pricing system based on shipment weight. Higher volume often gets lower rates per unit. Encourages consolidation of cargo to save costs. Helps carriers manage space and optimize revenue. Customers benefit from predictable pricing for different weight bands. Common in air and road freight.",
  "White-label Platform":
    "A software solution branded for a specific company but developed by another provider. Used by logistics companies for booking, tracking, or management. Enables customization without building technology from scratch. Supports client-facing portals or apps. Improves efficiency and brand presence. Reduces IT development costs.",
  "With Average (WA)":
    "A marine insurance term covering partial loss or damage to cargo. Unlike Total Loss coverage, it reimburses proportional losses. Policies define specific risks and limitations. Shippers pay premiums based on cargo value and coverage. It protects against minor accidents at sea. Standard practice in general cargo insurance.",
  //X Row
  "X-ray Scanning":
    "A security inspection method using X-rays to examine cargo contents. Detects prohibited items, explosives, or contraband. Used at airports, ports, and border checkpoints. Non-intrusive and fast for large volumes. Enhances safety and compliance in the supply chain. Helps prevent illegal or unsafe shipments.",
  //Y Row
  "York-Antwerp Rules of 1974":
    "International rules for sharing losses when cargo or ship is sacrificed at sea. Defines how General Average contributions are calculated. Ensures fair treatment of all parties involved. Widely used in marine insurance and claims settlements. Standardizes procedures for emergency situations. Protects shipowners and cargo owners financially.",
  //Z Row
  "Zone of Rate Flexibility":
    "A tariff range within which carriers can adjust freight rates freely. Provides flexibility to respond to market demand and cost changes. Helps maintain competitiveness without formal approvals. Shippers may see minor rate variations within the zone. Encourages efficient pricing strategies. It applies in both sea and air freight markets.",
  "Zone of Rate Freedom":
    "An area where carriers have full discretion to set freight rates. No regulatory limits within this zone. Encourages competition and market-driven pricing. Shippers can negotiate rates directly with carriers. It supports efficient supply chain cost management. Often applies to certain trade lanes or services.",
  "Zone of Reasonableness":
    "A concept in tariffs defining a fair range for rate adjustments. Prevents excessive pricing while allowing cost recovery. Ensures balanced charges for carriers and shippers. Used in regulated or semi-regulated transport markets. Supports transparent and predictable pricing. Helps maintain trust in trade practices.",
  "Zulu Time":
    "Another term for Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) used in aviation and shipping. Standardizes time reference worldwide. Avoids confusion across time zones for scheduling. Used in flight plans, tracking, and logistics documentation. Helps synchronize operations globally. Essential for international transport coordination.",
};

export default description;
